---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---
<Base
  title="mhs — Isosurfaces"
  description="Opaque level sets of three stress scalars around a rough crack, computed with a mollified kernel and with a classical triangular dislocation element, at two scales."
  current="isosurface"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    <h1>Isosurfaces<span class="sub">the field, asked for everywhere at once</span></h1>
    <p>
      A collocation scheme asks a kernel for traction at element centroids. A
      slice asks for stress on a plane. A <b>level set</b> asks for it
      everywhere and then draws the places where the answer equals a number
      &mdash; which is the sharpest form of the question, and the one picture
      the slice viewer cannot make.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same rough crack as the <a href={`${base}roughness/`}>Rough crack</a>
      page: 1044 elements, radius 6&nbsp;km, dipping 30&deg; at 20&nbsp;km,
      slipping to a peak of 0.27&nbsp;m. Three scalars, two levels each, both
      kernels, two scales.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      Colour here is <b>material and light, not data</b>. A level set carries
      one value of its scalar everywhere by definition, so there is nothing for
      a colour map to say; the shape is carried by silhouette and shading. The
      numbers are in the legend under the canvas.
    </div>
    <p class="contact">
      Switch <b>look</b> to <i>neutral</i> to see the same geometry under flat
      white light with no rim &mdash; the view that exists so the art direction
      can be checked rather than trusted.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <div id="viewer" class="iso">
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="scene"><span>scene</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="scalar"><span>scalar</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="level"><span>level</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="kernel"><span>kernel</span></label>
    </div>
    <div data-canvas class="canvas" aria-label="Opaque stress isosurfaces around a rough dipping crack, lit from three directions, with the crack's own triangulated surface inside them"></div>
    <p data-legend class="legend"></p>
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="crack"><span>crack</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="look"><span>look</span></label>
      <span class="lbl">view</span>
      <span class="btns" data-ctl="view"></span>
      <span class="btns" data-ctl="shot"></span>
      <label data-ctl="publication"><span>publication view</span></label>
    </div>
    <p data-status class="status">loading&hellip;</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What a level set is a test of</h2>
  <p>
    On the rough-crack page the classical triangular dislocation element comes
    out <i>well</i> at the one place a collocation scheme ever asks: an
    element&rsquo;s own centroid, which sits a fixed 0.29&nbsp;<i>h</i> from
    its own edges and so a fixed distance from the singularity of its bounding
    loop. It is finite there for that geometric reason, which is why it
    assembles a good interaction matrix and recovers Eshelby marginally better
    than the mollified kernel does. What it cannot do is hand you the field
    between the elements, or anywhere else on the fault &mdash; the
    <a href={`${base}onfault/`}>On the fault</a> page is that measurement. Two adjacent
    elements that slip by different amounts leave a residual dislocation on the
    edge they share, and its stress diverges as 1/<i>d</i> &mdash; on
    <b>1518 interior edges</b>, none of which is a physical feature of the
    crack.
  </p>
  <p>
    A level set is where that becomes visible rather than tabulated, because it
    is the one readout that asks the kernel for a value at every point at once.
    Where the two kernels agree, the two surfaces are the same surface. Where
    they do not, one of them wraps the mesh.
  </p>

  <h2>Two scales, because one grid cannot hold both</h2>
  <p>
    The classical field reaches a level <i>L</i> at a distance
    <i>r</i>&nbsp;&asymp;&nbsp;<i>A</i>/<i>L</i> from a shared edge, with
    <i>A</i>&nbsp;&asymp;&nbsp;2&times;10<sup>&minus;4</sup>&nbsp;GPa&nbsp;km
    measured on this mesh. At 0.05&nbsp;GPa that is <b>4&nbsp;m</b>. Resolving
    4&nbsp;m over a 12&nbsp;km crack is of order 10<sup>9</sup> grid points, or
    several hundred hours of kernel evaluation &mdash; so this page does not
    pretend to show the singular lines at full scale. It shows two honest
    things instead:
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>scene</th><th>box</th><th>spacing</th><th>points</th>
            <th>what it is for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>envelope</b></td><td>14 &times; 14 &times; 9 km</td>
            <td>0.2 km = 4 <i>eps</i></td><td>231,886</td>
            <td>the shape of the field around the whole crack, and how far
                apart the two kernels put the same level</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>detail</b></td><td>1.6 &times; 1.6 &times; 0.6 km</td>
            <td>0.02 km = 0.4 <i>eps</i></td><td>203,391</td>
            <td>one patch at 20&times; the resolution, fine enough for a
                shared edge&rsquo;s own structure to be several cells across</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The detail slab is centred on the interior edge carrying the largest slip
    mismatch on the whole crack, and it is oriented on the <i>local</i> mean
    plane of the facets it contains rather than on the crack&rsquo;s global mean
    plane: across a 2.4&nbsp;km footprint this surface wanders 0.47&nbsp;km about
    the global plane and 0.18&nbsp;km about the local one, so the global
    alignment would have sampled mostly empty half space and clipped the crack
    at both ends.
  </p>

  <h2>What the three scalars are</h2>
  <p>
    Stress is a tensor, and &ldquo;a stress isosurface&rdquo; means nothing
    until the scalar is named. All three here come from the same elastic stress
    tensor, with the mollified double layer&rsquo;s eigenstress subtracted, in
    GPa:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>von Mises</b>,
      &radic;(3/2&nbsp;<i>s<sub>ij</sub>s<sub>ij</sub></i>) on the deviator
      &mdash; an invariant, positive, the same quantity the slice viewer
      shows.</li>
    <li><b>Differential stress</b>,
      <i>&sigma;</i><sub>1</sub>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;<i>&sigma;</i><sub>3</sub>
      &mdash; also an invariant, and the one a rock-mechanics reader expects.</li>
    <li><b>&Delta;CFS</b> on a declared receiver: planes parallel to the
      crack&rsquo;s mean plane, slipping in the crack&rsquo;s mean slip
      direction, friction 0.6. Signed, so its two surfaces are the
      <b>+<i>L</i> and &minus;<i>L</i> lobes</b> rather than nested shells. The
      normal is tension positive and positive &Delta;CFS is toward failure; the
      sign is anchored by a test rather than by this sentence, and the anchor is
      that inside a slipping patch slip must <i>relieve</i> the Coulomb stress
      on planes parallel to it &mdash; measured here on 117 elements more than
      1.25&nbsp;km inside the patch, 100&nbsp;% negative, where an inverted slip
      direction would give 0&nbsp;%.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Levels are set by a rule rather than by eye: the 90th and 99th percentiles
    of the mollified scalar inside the box, and both kernels are then contoured
    at the same number &mdash; which is the only way the comparison means
    anything.
  </p>

  <h2>What the comparison measured</h2>
  <p>
    Both kernels were evaluated at all 231,886 points of the envelope grid, and
    contoured at the <i>same</i> levels &mdash; percentiles of the mollified
    field, so the classical surface is never handed a level chosen to flatter
    it. Two numbers come out of each pair. The <b>distance</b> is from every
    vertex of one surface to the other surface, point to surface rather than
    point to vertex, so a denser mesh cannot read as a closer one. The
    <b>area</b> is the discriminator the distance cannot be: two level sets at
    the same level enclose the same region or they do not.
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>level</th><th>median apart</th><th>worst apart</th>
            <th>mollified area</th><th>classical area</th><th>excess</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>von Mises p90</td><td>1.2 m <span class="dim">(0.006 cell)</span></td>
            <td>758 m <span class="dim">(3.8 cells)</span></td>
            <td>251.2 km<sup>2</sup></td><td>251.7 km<sup>2</sup></td>
            <td><b>+0.2 %</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>von Mises p99</td><td>9.3 m <span class="dim">(0.046 cell)</span></td>
            <td>498 m <span class="dim">(2.5 cells)</span></td>
            <td>121.4 km<sup>2</sup></td><td>130.2 km<sup>2</sup></td>
            <td><b>+7.3 %</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>&Delta;CFS +<i>L</i> lobe</td><td>31.6 m <span class="dim">(0.158 cell)</span></td>
            <td>1231 m <span class="dim">(6.2 cells)</span></td>
            <td>20.6 km<sup>2</sup></td><td>30.5 km<sup>2</sup></td>
            <td><b>+48 %</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    So the answer is not one word. Over nearly all of their area the two
    surfaces <i>are</i> the same surface: a median departure of 1.2&nbsp;m at
    p90 is six thousandths of a grid cell, and the two fields&rsquo; minima over
    the whole box agree to five digits
    (1.1168 against 1.1166&times;10<sup>&minus;5</sup>&nbsp;GPa) &mdash; the far
    field is not in question. The departures are local, and they are large where
    they occur: several cells, at isolated places, which is what a defect on a
    shared edge looks like from 0.2&nbsp;km away.
  </p>
  <p>
    The area says which kernel is the one moving, and the trend says why. Move
    the level from p90 into the tail and the classical surface inflates
    &mdash; 0.2&nbsp;%, then 7.3&nbsp;%, then 48&nbsp;% &mdash; because the tail
    of the classical field <i>is</i> the edge singularities. Taken over the
    whole box, the classical von Mises maximum is
    <b>0.217&nbsp;GPa against the mollified 0.0134</b>, sixteen times larger, on
    a grid whose cells are 4&nbsp;<i>eps</i> wide and which therefore comes
    nowhere near resolving the lines carrying it. That factor is a lower bound
    set by the grid, not a property of the field.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    One statistic was measured and <b>does not</b> discriminate: the number of
    separate pieces. A tail level set of a rough crack is dozens of lobes rather
    than one shell, and at von Mises p99 the <i>mollified</i> surface carries
    more of them than the classical one &mdash; 38 against 34. Fragmentation
    here is set by the roughness of the crack and by where it slips, not by the
    kernel, so the piece count is published as a description of what you are
    looking at and not as evidence.
  </div>

  <h2>And what it measured at twenty times the resolution</h2>
  <p>
    The detail slab samples at 0.02&nbsp;km &mdash; 0.4&nbsp;<i>eps</i>, ten
    times finer than the envelope &mdash; and there the two kernels are not the
    same object at all:
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>level</th><th>median apart</th><th>worst apart</th>
            <th>mollified area</th><th>classical area</th><th>excess</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>von Mises p90</td><td>18.1 m <span class="dim">(0.90 cell)</span></td>
            <td>394 m <span class="dim">(19.7 cells)</span></td>
            <td>3.70 km<sup>2</sup></td><td>5.26 km<sup>2</sup></td>
            <td><b>+42 %</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>von Mises p99</td><td>58.6 m <span class="dim">(2.93 cells)</span></td>
            <td>448 m <span class="dim">(22.4 cells)</span></td>
            <td>0.93 km<sup>2</sup></td><td>3.09 km<sup>2</sup></td>
            <td><b>+232 %</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>&Delta;CFS +<i>L</i> lobe</td><td>161 m <span class="dim">(8.1 cells)</span></td>
            <td>965 m <span class="dim">(48.2 cells)</span></td>
            <td>0.13 km<sup>2</sup></td><td>1.30 km<sup>2</sup></td>
            <td><b>+918 %</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The field itself says the same thing more bluntly. On these same points,
    from this same slip, von Mises runs to <b>0.0142&nbsp;GPa</b> under the
    mollified kernel and <b>4.11&nbsp;GPa</b> under the classical one &mdash; a
    factor of 290. And the mollified maximum barely moved from the
    envelope&rsquo;s 0.0134: a bounded field does not care what grid you ask it
    on. The classical maximum went from 0.217 to 4.11&nbsp;GPa for the same
    refinement, because what it is reporting is not a feature of the crack but
    how close the grid came to a line.
  </p>

  <h2>The tubes, predicted before they were looked at</h2>
  <p>
    A residual dislocation on a shared edge gives a 1/<i>d</i> field, so a level
    <i>L</i> should be reached at <i>r</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<i>A</i>/<i>L</i> and the
    classical level set should wrap that edge in a tube of radius <i>r</i>. That
    is a falsifiable statement, and it was written down before the surfaces were
    extracted: 3.4 to 7.6 cells at these levels, which is why the slab is
    sampled this finely. Measured afterwards off the extracted geometry, as the
    median standoff along the middle of the edge the slab is centred on:
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>level</th><th>predicted <i>r</i></th><th>measured</th>
            <th>ratio</th><th>mollified, same edge</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>von Mises p90 <span class="dim">6.40 MPa</span></td>
            <td>112.7 m</td><td>78.1 m</td><td>0.69</td><td>15.3 m</td></tr>
        <tr><td>von Mises p99 <span class="dim">9.30 MPa</span></td>
            <td>77.5 m</td><td>51.9 m</td><td>0.67</td><td>32.9 m</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&sigma;<sub>1</sub>&minus;&sigma;<sub>3</sub> p90 <span class="dim">7.28 MPa</span></td>
            <td>99.1 m</td><td>79.2 m</td><td>0.80</td><td>15.3 m</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&sigma;<sub>1</sub>&minus;&sigma;<sub>3</sub> p99 <span class="dim">10.59 MPa</span></td>
            <td>68.1 m</td><td>52.2 m</td><td>0.77</td><td>33.0 m</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The tubes are there, and they are about two thirds to four fifths of the
    predicted radius. The prefactor <i>A</i> is the weak half of that
    prediction &mdash; it was measured at one edge whose slip mismatch is 3.6
    times smaller and scaled linearly &mdash; so the law is tested separately
    from it. <b>r&nbsp;&prop;&nbsp;1/<i>L</i> holds to 3.4&nbsp;% on von Mises
    and 4.0&nbsp;% on differential stress</b>: the ratio of the two measured
    radii is 0.664 and 0.660 against the 0.687 that the two levels alone
    predict, with no fitted quantity anywhere in it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The mollified surface is the control, and it behaves in the opposite sense.
    Its standoff from that same edge <i>grows</i> with the level, 15.3&nbsp;m to
    32.9&nbsp;m, where a tube must shrink as 1/<i>L</i>. It is not wrapping the
    edge; it does not know the edge is there, and its position is set by the
    smooth field around it.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    The tube rule is <b>not</b> applicable to &Delta;CFS, and the measurement
    says so rather than being quietly omitted: both lobes come within
    13.1&nbsp;m of the edge, under one cell, against a predicted 152&nbsp;m. The
    1/<i>d</i> coefficient of a <i>signed</i> scalar changes sign with azimuth
    around the line, so there is no radius at which the field reaches
    +<i>L</i> all the way round. Both lobes run into the line instead of
    standing off it &mdash; which is why they measure the same 13.1&nbsp;m.
  </div>

  <h2>How much to trust the surfaces</h2>
  <p>
    Each surface is checked against the field it claims to come from: every
    vertex of the undecimated contour is compared with the trilinearly
    interpolated grid, and the worst departure from the requested level is
    reported in the manifest. Across all 24 published surfaces that worst
    departure is <b>0.11&nbsp;%</b>. The check is not a tautology, and the
    selfcheck proves it on a closed form by breaking it on purpose: against an
    analytic ellipsoid the contour sits on its level to 3&times;10<sup>&minus;5</sup>&nbsp;%,
    while a <i>y</i>&harr;<i>z</i> transposed grid reads 44&nbsp;% off, an
    <i>x</i>&harr;<i>z</i> transposed one 100&nbsp;%, and a 10&nbsp;% error in
    the grid spacing 56&nbsp;%.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing here was decimated or smoothed. The budget is 60,000 triangles and
    the largest surface on the page is 38,434, so every published mesh is the
    contour as it came out, and every decimation deviation in the manifest is
    exactly zero. That is deliberate: a smoothing pass is precisely what would
    remove a structure a few cells across and manufacture the agreement this
    page is testing.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    One grid node in the classical detail field &mdash; <b>1 of 203,391</b>
    &mdash; came out non-finite, where the point landed close enough to an
    element edge for the kernel&rsquo;s logarithms to overflow. The field really
    is unbounded there, so rather than invent a value, that node is given the
    largest-magnitude value among its six neighbours: because the field grows
    toward the edge, that puts it on the side of the level it is genuinely on.
    Every surface&rsquo;s manifest entry carries how many nodes this happened
    to. The mollified field has none, at either scale.
  </div>
  <p class="cap">
    The meshes are binary glTF in kilometres, <i>z</i> up and depth negative,
    with no transform node &mdash; the same files this page loads will open in
    Blender, which declares <i>y</i> up and will want one rotation. Every
    surface carries its scene, kernel, scalar, level, triangle count and sha256
    in <a href={`${base}data/iso_manifest.json`}>iso_manifest.json</a>. See
    <a href={`${base}roughness/`}>Rough crack</a> for the measurement this
    picture is of, and <a href={`${base}concepts/`}>Concepts</a> for what
    <i>eps</i> is.
  </p>

  <script>
    import { startIso } from "../viz/iso";
    startIso(document.getElementById("viewer")!, import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
  </script>
</Base>
